WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.383
Scenario: A QR code on a meeting-room poster promises

2
00:00:03.383 --> 00:00:06.766
access to the conference Wi-Fi. After scanning, it opens

3
00:00:06.766 --> 00:00:10.525
a sign-in page on a domain unrelated to the venue.

4
00:00:10.525 --> 00:00:13.908
Safest response: Option C: Check the displayed URL, close

5
00:00:13.908 --> 00:00:17.667
the page, and ask facilities/IT for the official network instructions.

6
00:00:17.667 --> 00:00:21.050
Verify the destination and obtain connection details through an

7
00:00:21.050 --> 00:00:24.433
approved internal source. Option A is unsafe because A

8
00:00:24.433 --> 00:00:28.191
different browser still submits credentials to the same untrusted site.

9
00:00:28.191 --> 00:00:31.574
Option B is unsafe because The physical location does

10
00:00:31.574 --> 00:00:34.957
not validate the QR destination or page operator. Option

11
00:00:34.957 --> 00:00:38.340
D is unsafe because Sharing credentials exposes the coworker’s

12
00:00:38.340 --> 00:00:41.723
account and does not validate the page. In practice:

13
00:00:41.723 --> 00:00:45.106
Do not enter credentials. Confirm the poster with facilities

14
00:00:45.106 --> 00:00:48.489
or IT and use the authorized network onboarding process.

15
00:00:48.489 --> 00:00:51.872
Key takeaway: QR codes conceal destinations; assess the actual

16
00:00:51.872 --> 00:00:53.000
URL before authenticating.
